.TH iron-vault-reset 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-reset \- Discard the vault and the config, so `init` can make a new vault: the way out when no passphrase, recovery key or approver works. It asks for no owner factor, only a confirmation typed at a terminal, which is the vault ID, or the state directory\*(Aqs path when no vault is Initialized. It refuses a pipe, a file or a missing `/dev/tty` with exit code 3 before it connects. The service locks first, then removes the store\*(Aqs files and the config, keeps any foreign file, and ends Locked. Nothing can be recovered afterwards: keep your own copy of the config
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault reset\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Discard the vault and the config, so `init` can make a new vault: the way out when no passphrase, recovery key or approver works. It asks for no owner factor, only a confirmation typed at a terminal, which is the vault ID, or the state directory\*(Aqs path when no vault is Initialized. It refuses a pipe, a file or a missing `/dev/tty` with exit code 3 before it connects. The service locks first, then removes the store\*(Aqs files and the config, keeps any foreign file, and ends Locked. Nothing can be recovered afterwards: keep your own copy of the config
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
